Catalogue SafeR = (Catalogue)R.RemoveField("Itemcode"): Explanation: For "new" requests, do not take the "identity" input: strip it, assign automatically. It is only for the backend function.

Date fields: Use date controls. Use Now as the default, and when a range is expected, use Now minus 180 days in the past for the "earlier" date. Make sure everything cleanly serializes to JSON native types. Use the public DB type as the reference, it is auto-generated from the DB (dotnet ef scaffold) and the target is Postgres; the provider is NPgSql. I think for new records it is better to hide the ID column until reset. So add: New, Load, Edit, Save, Cancel, Reset buttons, with stacklayout, center them and remember to set the height and width of the stacklayout from ColorSettings as it impacts performance in Core.System.Windows.Forms (the Mono-based port). Cancel should not be wired to anything else, just reset the current edit. Gridviews: you should make it yourself. You will know when to use it. E.g. you have an invoice and a list of items, etc. Place the buttons on top, inside the panel, and have well defined heights and widths as it is important for the performance of Eto.Forms in Core.System.Windows.Forms (the dotnet core version that uses libgdiplus for drawing, the native Linux port).

Date range example: Searching accounting entries by date. They should have a [Start] and [End] date.

Also: for localization: use
string Text = TranslationHelper.Translate(
"Username"
)
should give "Username" if not found, otherwise the translation. No translation for user-entered fields, just labels. For translation keys, use the label; it will be translated _later_ otherwise will fallback. New resets after a confirmation "Yes". Let the TableLayout column count be configurable, default to 2. The DTO is the EF type. EF type should have information redacted at backend, and clearly mark which fields need to be redacted (like e.g. ID when creating a new entry, ID should not be editable on edit, etc., so should remind me to redact them at the bottom in the backend). Double check against Eto.Forms docs to check for ExpandContentWidth/ExpandContentHeight settings and whether they apply to a container. Localization always works well. Console.WriteLine is ok for Log.

EF = DTO, will be given to you. Let me know if you understand.

TableLayout does not contain any "ColumnSpacing", "ExpandContentWidth", "ExpandContentHeight". It does not support col/row spans. TableCell does not have "Expand". Use e.g. ColorSettings.InnerLabelWidth ?? [sensible width] instead of ColorSettings.InnerLabelWidth for assignment.

'TextBox', or any control, do not contain a definition for 'Name'. EventHandler<System.EventArgs> over EventHandler for events. It is not CommonUi.SearchPanelUtility, just SearchPanelUtility. Explicitly convert things to StackLayoutItem if StackLayout using LINQ. Don't set the items (is read-only), use the constructor that takes StackLayoutItem[] (dont put orientation there, dont use bare controls, orientation doesnt belong to constructor, is a property). Columns count are not just for spacing, it decides whether 1, 2, or 3 of the inputs are shown in one line. Wire a parameterless constructor with defaults. Group control declarations if you can. Panels don't have ForegroundColor, CheckBox.Checked is bool not bool?. NA fields: stub with anonymous classes, wire search. No builtin columncount, write layouting loop yourself than manual (with columncount as parameter). FocusNavigationDirection doesnt exist. dont assume List<Vendors> exists, use anonymous classes with dummy data. Make sure e.g. 4 ColumnCount show 4 inputs on the same line, not just spacing.

Say whether you can parse this or not, don't write any code.

Now the next thing is: develop a CalculateDiscounts method. Discount can be additive, multiplicative, or the "highest" which is the setting for controlling the precedence. Discount should never be so high to the point that Selling Price - Discount is less than the minimum price and the Loyalty Point obtained to the current item. I will give you the EF core classes. EF core classes are ALSO THE SAME CLASSES for data exchange with the client, except for a few exceptions (so we reuse things to the max). Also write a CalculateLoyaltyPoints method. The table Pii contains user-specific discounts and the loyalty points obtaining rate. Pii stands for: Personally Identifiable Information. It is handy when we need to attend to GDPR and the likes. Moreover, there is also volume discounts, which is item-specific and depends on the quantity ordered. Then there is... Promotional Discount that only applies to a particular batch/item. If a batch has a percentage or value set for discount, use that, but if not, use the catalogueitem's one (prefer the most specificity). There are also flags indicating whether some discounts would apply in some circumstances. Remember to make these things take arguments and make them pure. They could take: something in LINQ that comes BEFORE ToList() as ToList() could be expensive and the query would be sent to the server. Construct LINQ and return LINQ expressions until the last bit. Keep all of them as LINQ queryables, just tell me what are the things needed. DON'T REWRITE ANY CLASSES.

